  <p class="note">{% if b.print_mode %}The interactive HTML version of this bulletin can be zoomed to the full 10 m detail, with each model's map, the model agreement and village details. {% else %}The flood map is drawn at its full 10 m detail; zoom in to see it. Dots, lines and labels keep the same size on the screen at every zoom. {% endif %}At the whole-corridor view the coloured circles mark each 1 km square with mapped flood, sized by its area; they hide once you zoom in. Hillshade from the Copernicus GLO-30 DEM.</p>
</div>
<div class="about"><b>What this map shows and how it is made.</b> Sentinel-1 sends radar pulses to the ground and measures what comes back. Calm water reflects the pulse away from the satellite, so it looks dark. Each model looks at the dark patches together with the height above the river (HAND) and the slope, and gives every 10 m cell a chance of being water. Cells above the model's threshold count as water; water that is there in most years (the normal river) and slopes the radar cannot see are then taken out. What is left is the mapped flood. The trust colour of a cell comes from how often the model's flood calls were right on the same kind of ground on the two checked events.</div>
{% if b.insets %}
<h3>Where the most flood is mapped</h3>
<p class="note">The three 3 km by 3 km squares with the most mapped flood, at full detail. {% if not b.print_mode %}Click a close-up to zoom the main map there.{% endif %}</p>
<div class="insets">
{% for i in b.insets %}<div class="inset" data-box="{{ loop.index0 }}"{% if not b.print_mode %} onclick="gjBox({{ loop.index0 }})" title="Zoom the main map to {{ i.key }}"{% endif %}>
