</div>{% endfor %}
</div>{% endif %}

<h2>Flood area by reach and trust</h2>
<div class="about"><b>What this shows.</b> The corridor is split at Devprayag into two reaches that behave differently: the narrow gorge above Devprayag and the wider open valley below it, where the river becomes the Ganga. The table gives the mapped flood in each reach, split by trust grade.</div>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Reach</th><th class="r">High trust (km&sup2;)</th><th class="r">Medium (km&sup2;)</th><th class="r">Low (km&sup2;)</th><th class="r">Total (km&sup2;)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for r in b.reach_rows %}<tr{% if r.total_row %} class="head-row"{% endif %}><td>{{ r.name }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.high }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.med }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.low }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.total }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}</tbody>
</table>
<p class="note">Trust grades: {% for k in [3, 2, 1, 0] %}<b>{{ b.trust_plain[k].split(": ")[0] }}</b>: {{ b.trust_plain[k].split(": ", 1)[1] }}{{ "; " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %}</p>

<h2>Where the models agree</h2>
<div class="about"><b>What this shows.</b> Three different models map every pass: a Random Forest (many decision trees voting), a U-Net (a neural network that looks at the neighbourhood of each cell) and TerraMind (a large model pretrained on satellite images, fine-tuned here). The first table counts the area called flood by one, two or all three. The second lists each model's own flood area, its threshold (the water probability above which a cell counts as water) and how much was taken out as normal river water or as radar blind. {% if not b.print_mode %}Each model's map and the agreement map can be switched on under Layers on the map.{% endif %}</div>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Models calling a cell flood</th><th class="r">Area (km&sup2;)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{% for r in b.agree_rows %}<tr><td>{{ r.label }}</td><td class="r">{{ r.km2 }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
</table>
<table style="margin-top:10px">
  <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Training set</th><th class="r">Threshold</th><th class="r">Flood area (km&sup2;)</th><th class="r">Removed as permanent water (km&sup2;)</th><th class="r">Removed as radar blind (km&sup2;)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{% for f in b.family_rows %}<tr{% if f.headline %} class="head-row"{% endif %}><td>{{ f.name }}{% if f.headline %} (headline){% endif %}</td><td>{{ f.trainset }}</td><td class="r">{{ f.threshold }}</td><td class="r">{{ f.flood }}</td><td class="r">{{ f.perm }}</td><td class="r">{{ f.distort }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
</table>
<p class="note">A cell called flood by every model is the most dependable part of the map. Areas called flood by one model only are worth checking on the ground before acting on them.</p>

{% if b.forecast %}<h2>River forecast before this pass</h2>
<div class="about"><b>What this shows and how it works.</b> GloFAS (the Global Flood Awareness System of the Copernicus Emergency Management Service) forecasts river flow every day, up to 30 days ahead, as a set of 51 forecasts. For each of five gauge points on the corridor, the row below shows what the forecasts issued 14 days to 0 days before this pass said about the radar day. A square is <b>w</b> (watch) when at least half of the 51 forecasts were above the 2-year flow, and <b>W</b> (warning) when at least half were above the 5-year flow. The 2-year flow is the flow that GloFAS's own 47-year record reaches on average once in two years. Hover over a square for the middle forecast flow. This is a look back at a published forecast, not a warning, and GloFAS flows on a 5 km grid are not gauge readings.</div>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Gauge point</th><th class="r">2-year flow (m&sup3;/s)</th><th class="r">5-year flow (m&sup3;/s)</th><th>Forecast issued 14 days before &#8594; on the day</th><th>GloFAS flow on the day</th><th class="r">Gauge level (m)</th><th>Above CWC warning level</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{% for r in b.forecast %}<tr><td>{{ r.station }}</td><td class="r">{{ "{:,.0f}".format(r.rp2) if r.rp2 is not none else "n/a" }}</td><td class="r">{{ "{:,.0f}".format(r.rp5) if r.rp5 is not none else "n/a" }}</td>
  <td style="white-space:nowrap">{% for d in r.days %}<span class="fc" style="background:{{ b.forecast_cols.get(d.status, b.forecast_cols.unknown) if d.status else '#e5e7eb' }}" title="{{ d.k }} day{{ 's' if d.k != 1 else '' }} before: {{ d.label }}{% if d.q is not none %}, middle forecast {{ '{:,.0f}'.format(d.q) }} m3/s{% endif %}">{{ "W" if d.status == "warning" else ("w" if d.status == "watch" else "") }}</span>{% endfor %}</td>
  <td>{% if r.rean_q is not none %}{{ "{:,.0f}".format(r.rean_q) }} m&sup3;/s, {% endif %}{{ r.rean_status }}</td><td class="r">{{ "%.2f"|format(r.stage) if r.stage is not none else "n/a" }}</td><td>{{ r.above }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}</tbody>
</table>
<p class="legend"><span><span class="fc" style="background:{{ b.forecast_cols.none }}"></span>below the 2-year flow</span><span><span class="fc" style="background:{{ b.forecast_cols.watch }}">w</span>watch</span><span><span class="fc" style="background:{{ b.forecast_cols.warning }}">W</span>warning</span><span><span class="fc" style="background:#e5e7eb"></span>no forecast that day</span></p>
<p class="note">{{ b.forecast[0].versions }}: each forecast is read with the thresholds of the GloFAS version that made it. "GloFAS flow on the day" is the GloFAS {{ b.forecast[0].rean_version }} reanalysis, the model's best estimate after the event. Gauge level: the daily mean water level on the radar day, in metres above the gauge zero, from the GUARDIAN gauge series (it ends in July 2024, so later passes have no data); "above CWC warning level" compares it with the gauge's published warning level. Source: Copernicus Emergency Management Service, GloFAS (CC BY 4.0); worked out by Jal-SAR extension M9.</p>
{% endif %}

